If a is a closure, a fixed point x is compact if and only if it equals the fixed point generated by e n for some n.

If x is compact, the retract basis is directed and its supremum is x, so some basis element lies below x. That element is the generated fixed point at its index, and monotonicity of application of a, rewritten by x being fixed, gives the reverse inequality from the containment of e n. Conversely, every generated fixed point is compact.
